Eight-Process Synthesis - Algebraic MINLP for BARON
C o m p i l a t i o n


   2   
      Algebraic MINLP reformulation of the classical eight-process
      synthesis problem used in GAMS LOGMIP3.
       
      Original references:
      Duran, M.A. PhD thesis, Carnegie Mellon University, 1984.
       
      Turkay, M.; Grossmann, I.E.
      Logic-based MINLP algorithms for the optimal synthesis of process networks.
      Computers & Chemical Engineering 20 (1996) 959-978.
       
      Purpose of this version:
      Deterministic global MINLP benchmark for comparison with CORAL-R1.
       
      Revision note:
      This publication-ready version removes the artificial blanket bound
      X.up(I)=20 used in an earlier comparison model. Finite stream bounds are
      instead derived analytically from the canonical LOGMIP3 equations, explicit
      bounds, and active/off disjunctions. Unit-specific big-M constants are then
      derived from those valid bounds.
  24   
  25   
  26  Set
  27      I 'process streams' /1*25/
  28      J 'process units'   /1*8/;
  29   
  30   
  31  Parameter CV(I) 'variable cost coefficients'
  32         /  3 = -10
  33            5 = -15
  34            9 = -40
  35           19 =  25
  36           21 =  35
  37           25 = -35
  38           17 =  80
  39           14 =  15
  40           10 =  15
  41            2 =   1
  42            4 =   1
  43           18 = -65
  44           20 = -60
  45           22 = -80 /;
  46   
  47   
  48  Parameters
  49      UB(I) 'analytically valid upper bounds for process streams'
  50      BM(J) 'unit-specific big-M constants for disjunctive equations';
  51   
  52  * Bounds below are implied by the canonical LOGMIP3 model.
  53  * They replace the artificial blanket bound X.up(I)=20 used in an earlier
  54  * comparison model. No feasible integer solution of the canonical model is
  55  * excluded by these bounds.
  56  UB('1')  = exp(2)-1 + exp(2/1.2)-1;
  57  UB('2')  = exp(2)-1;
  58  UB('3')  = 2;
  59  UB('4')  = exp(2/1.2)-1;
  60  UB('5')  = 2;
  61  UB('6')  = 4;
  62  UB('7')  = 4;
  63  UB('8')  = 4;
  64  UB('9')  = 2;
  65  UB('10') = 1;
  66  UB('11') = 4;
  67  UB('12') = 4;
  68  UB('13') = 4;
  69  UB('14') = 1;
  70  UB('15') = 4;
  71  UB('16') = 2;
  72  UB('17') = 2;
  73  UB('18') = log(4);
  74  UB('19') = 2;
  75  UB('20') = 1.5*log(3);
  76  UB('21') = 2;
  77  UB('22') = log(3);
  78  UB('23') = 1.5*log(3) + log(3);
  79  UB('24') = 1.5*log(3) + log(3);
  80  UB('25') = 3;
  81   
  82  * Valid residual bounds over the variable box above. These constants are
  83  * used only to deactivate the corresponding process equation when Y(j)=0.
  84  BM('1') = exp(2)-1;
  85  BM('2') = exp(2/1.2)-1;
  86  BM('3') = 4;
  87  BM('4') = 6.25;
  88  BM('5') = 4;
  89  BM('6') = 2;
  90  BM('7') = 2;
  91  BM('8') = 3;
  92   
  93   
  94  Variable
  95      PROF 'objective';
  96   
  97  Binary Variable
  98      Y(J) 'process-unit selection';
  99   
 100  Positive Variable
 101      X(I)  'process streams'
 102      CF(J) 'fixed costs';
